## M3 technical design

Keep the deterministic engine unchanged. Introduce one student-facing view model:

PersonalGuidanceView
├── simple_summary
├── verified_results
├── missing_or_uncertain_information
├── things_worth_checking
├── explanation
├── evidence
└── next_action

The UI should render this contract instead of exposing raw GuidanceResult, rule IDs or backend field names.

Every result page should answer:

What does this mean for me?
What is still unknown?
Why did I get this result?
What should I do next?

## Architecture guidance

Keep the current TypeScript modular monolith.

Do not yet:

Rewrite it in Next.js.
Add Supabase or user accounts.
Build production RAG.
Add admission prediction.
Expand beyond the five-college pilot.
Build WhatsApp integration.

Add infrastructure only when validated student needs require it.

## Essential engineering improvements

Commit a package lockfile and define the exact Node version.
Add CI for tests, type checking and data validation.
Replace or strengthen the simple comma-splitting CSV parser before importing complex data.
Add request-size limits to every POST endpoint.
Add security headers and HTML-escaping tests.
Never log marks, certificates or profiles.
Create mobile browser tests for the three primary journeys.
Resolve the README/project-status contradiction.

## Data direction

Maintain two clearly separated stores:

Stable guidance content: awareness, branch explanations and basic TNEA concepts.
Time-sensitive facts: dates, documents, vacancies, seats and counselling updates.

Every time-sensitive fact should have source, page/section, applicable year, verification date and expiry/review date. Missing facts must remain explicitly unknown.

## Role split

The engineer should own:

Product decisions.
Source approval.
Tamil quality review.
Security/deployment approval.
Final student acceptance.

Codex should handle:

Implementation drafts.
Tests and scenario fixtures.
Refactoring and validation scripts.
Documentation consistency.
Data-quality checks.
Review checklists.

The immediate next action should be the bounded M3 presentation work described in docs/planning/implementation_plan_v1.md, followed immediately by a small student-observation round. That is the shortest path from “technically correct prototype” to “mission-serving product.”


